WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:07.680
Speaker 1: Red states across the country are&nbsp;
continuing to pass restrictive abortion measures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.840
with the idea that they can be challenged up to&nbsp;
the high court or that they can just be outright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.800
allowed to stand if and when Roe versus Wade is&nbsp;
overturned. The latest such example comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.280
Louisiana with a bill that they have passed,&nbsp;
Governor John Bel Edwards, who is a Democrat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:27.040
mind you, but a so-called pro-life Democrat,&nbsp;
he has signed into law Senate Bill 342,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.160
which is a trigger law that will do away with&nbsp;
abortion in almost all circumstances. It does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.200
carve out exceptions for medical futility and&nbsp;
ectopic. Pregnancies, but it does not carve out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.320
exceptions for rape and incest. There's also&nbsp;
a penalty in there for abortion providers with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.680
sentences ranging anywhere from 10 to 15 years in&nbsp;
prison. Luckily, there is no criminal penalty for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.200
women who seek and get abortions in the state,&nbsp;
as has been the trend in many of these bills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.600
in red states across the country lately. The&nbsp;
governor said that he wishes that there was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.000
exception for rape and incest, but he decided to&nbsp;
sign the bill into law anyway. He explained this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.040
in a statement saying, quote, I am pro-life and&nbsp;
have never hidden from that fact. This does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.800
belie my belief that there should be an exception&nbsp;
to the prohibition on abortion for victims of rape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.880
and incest. However. Vetoing Senate Bill 342 would&nbsp;
not accomplish that end. In fact, vetoing Senate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.160
Bill 342 would leave fewer exceptions in place&nbsp;
than if the bill becomes law and would further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.640
confuse whether pregnancy begins at fertilization&nbsp;
or implantation. For these reasons, I have signed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.680
Senate Bill 342 into law. Now, I thought that&nbsp;
this story offered an opportunity to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.920
some of the Leafs in logic when it comes to the&nbsp;
right wing view on abortion, particularly when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.440
comes to not prosecuting women who have abortions,&nbsp;
and also when it comes to carving out exceptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.880
for rape and incest. So starting with the first&nbsp;
one, most Republicans at this point don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.520
see women prosecuted who have abortions. They&nbsp;
may want to prosecute the medical providers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.840
but not the women themselves. And to me, that&nbsp;
doesn't really make sense given their logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.240
right? Because if you buy into this idea that&nbsp;
abortion is murder, well, shouldn't you hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.000
accountable not only the person who was actually&nbsp;
doing the murder, but also the person who called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.320
the hit? Like if in this current society, as&nbsp;
things are, not only does a hit man get in trouble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.640
and get prosecuted if they kill someone, also the&nbsp;
person that ordered the hit gets in trouble as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:27.120
well. So why wouldn't the same be true for a case&nbsp;
having to do with an abortion? Also, the leap in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.160
logic when it comes to Republicans on this issue&nbsp;
has to do with the rape and incest thing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.400
they argue that life begins at conception. All&nbsp;
life is sacred. God has a plan for everyone. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.680
would that not apply to a woman who gets raped?&nbsp;
Now, certainly I don't think she should have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.960
carry a baby to term and have that be a constant&nbsp;
reminder of the tragic event that happened to her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.480
But many of these Republicans think that there&nbsp;
should be these exceptions carved out. And to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.000
it doesn't really seem to make sense if the&nbsp;
whole reason that they're against abortion is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.360
because they believe that life begins&nbsp;
at conception and they believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.800
it's murder to kill a baby in the womb. So given&nbsp;
their logic on these different things, to me, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:12.080
just doesn't make all that much sense. And sadly,&nbsp;
no longer are these conversations hypothetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.640
merely because now we're starting to see abortion&nbsp;
get banned in states across the country. Certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.920
that's going to be the case if Roe versus Wade is&nbsp;
indeed overturned. These trigger laws are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:27.440
get enacted like this one in Louisiana. And we're&nbsp;
going to have to have these conversations. And to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.640
my understanding, Republicans simply aren't ready&nbsp;
to have them. So that's the latest on one of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.120
abortion bills that are being passed in a red&nbsp;
state. And I'm sure there are more to come.

